description:
Problem: Prefix/KV caching delivers big cost savings but tuning TTLs and batching parameters is fragile across workloads. We need an automated advisor that recommends per-prefix TTLs and batching profiles with explicit confidence levels, plus a safe apply/rollback flow for config changes. Goal: reduce per-token cost by >=12% on sampled production traffic with <1% added latency tail and zero customer-visible correctness regressions.

Scope: build a confidence-driven model that synthesizes: 1) per-prefix TTL suggestions with an uncertainty score, 2) batching profiles (max batch size, latency budget) correlated with prefix characteristics, and 3) a staged apply + rollback orchestration that integrates with the runtime's canary APIs and rollover policies. The advisor will emit a proposed config bundle and a safety checklist for human review.

Deliverables:
- offline hit-rate simulator and estimator (sampling harness) with bootstrap CI notebooks;
- TTL recommendation algorithm that blends empirical hit-curves + Bayesian shrinkage to express confidence;
- batching suggestion engine that maps prefix signal (qps, seq len, prefix entropy) to batching parameters and expected cost/latency tradeoffs;
- staged apply playbook integrated with our rollout service (canary, shadow, dry-run, automatic rollback on predefined guardrails);
- dashboard widgets showing expected savings, confidence bands, and simulation traces.

Artifacts and links: design doc: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1A2b-ttl-batching-advisor-design; experiment notebook: https://colab.research.google.com/drive/ttl-batching-sim; PR prototype: https://github.com/redwood-inference/runtime/pull/5893; dashboard draft: https://console.redwood.ai/dashboards/ttl-advisor-sim

Success criteria:
- simulation shows >=12% median token-cost reduction across three representative services (chat, summarization, rerank);
- canary rollback time < 15 minutes for failing guardrail;
- advisor confidence correlates with actual hit-rate error (calibration within ±6 percentage points);
- operator UX accepts >80% of high-confidence recommendations in beta.

Notes: This ticket focuses on the advisor and the safe rollout integration. Model-serving runtime changes (kernel selection for batching) are covered in dependent ENG-271828. The advisor must not change runtime defaults without explicit opt-in and must provide an immediate revert key for all config bundles.
